e makes the required configuration impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The non\u2011sunny line is vertical.\n\nThe only vertical lines meeting \\(\\mathcal P\\) are  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad x=2,\\qquad x=3 .\n\\]\n\n*If the line is \\(x=1\\):* it contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nThe remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nR_v=\\{(2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line joining two points of \\(R_v\\) is either horizontal \\((y=1)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) (the line through \\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\)).  \nThus a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(R_v\\) unless it also passes through a point of \\(x=1\\).\n\nTake a sunny line through \\((2,1)\\) and a point of \\(x=1\\).  \nThe possibilities are the lines through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) with slopes \\(0,-1,-2\\).  \nOnly the last one,\n\n\\[\nL_{21}: y=-2x+5,\n\\]\n\nis sunny; it also contains \\((1,3)\\).  \n\nNow the uncovered points are \\((3,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  \nA sunny line through \\((3,1)\\) and a point of \\(x=1\\) must have slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) (with \\((1,2)\\)) or \\(-\\tfrac13\\) (with \\((1,3)\\)).  \nBoth are admissible, e.g.\n\n\\[\nL_{31}: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  \nAgain \\((2,2)\\) is left uncovered.  \n\nAll other choices are symmetric and lead to the same dead end; consequently a vertical non\u2011sunny line cannot yield a valid configuration.\n\n*If the vertical line is \\(x=2\\) or \\(x=3\\):* it contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\), leaving at least four points for the two sunny lines, and the same obstruction as above appears.  \n\nThus a vertical non\u2011sunny line is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The non\u2011sunny line has slope \\(-1\\).\n\nLines of the form \\(x+y=c\\) that meet \\(\\mathcal P\\) are  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\; x+y=3,\\; x+y=4 .\n\\]\n\n*If the line is \\(x+y=2\\):* it contains only \\((1,1)\\).  \nWe would then have to cover the remaining five points with two sunny lines, which is impossible because any sunny line can contain at most two of those points (any two of them are either vertical, horizontal, or have slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n*If the line is \\(x+y=3\\):* it contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \nThe uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\nR_{-1}=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAgain any line joining two points of \\(R_{-1}\\) is either vertical, horizontal, or has slope \\(-1\\).  \nHence a sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(R_{-1}\\) unless it also passes through \\((1,2)\\) or \\((2,1)\\).\n\nSuppose a sunny line \\(L\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and also \\((1,2)\\).  \nThe line through these two points is vertical, not sunny.  \nIf \\(L\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) it is horizontal, also not sunny.  \nThus any sunny line that meets the non\u2011sunny line \\(x+y=3\\) can cover at most one new point of \\(R_{-1}\\).  \nWith two sunny lines we can therefore cover at most two points of \\(R_{-1}\\), leaving at least two points uncovered.  \n\n*If the line is \\(x+y=4\\):* it contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nAny line through two of these three points is either vertical or has slope \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain at most one of them unless it also passes through a point of \\(x+y=4\\).  \nA sunny line through \\((1,2)\\) and a point of \\(x+y=4\\) must be the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\),\n\n\\[\nL_{12}: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\n\\]\n\nwhich is sunny.  \nA sunny line through \\((2,1)\\) and a point of \\(x+y=4\\) must be the line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\),\n\n\\[\nL_{21}: y=-2x+5,\n\\]\n\nalso sunny.  \nThese two lines together cover \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) but **do not** cover \\((1,1)\\).  \nThus the set cannot be covered with only two sunny lines.\n\nConsequently a non\u2011sunny line of slope \\(-1\\) also leads to impossibility.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible choice for the unique non\u2011sunny line forces at least one of the six required points to remain uncovered, no configuration of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, }k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]"
