rete specification after the claim?\"\n  - If Category A or B (zero details/type only) → INVALID METHOD → FAIL Level 2\n  - If Category C (one or more concrete details) → VALID METHOD → PASS Level 2, proceed to Level 3\n\n- **Level 3 (Presentation Quality) asks:** \"Are the provided details COMPLETE?\"\n  - Justification gaps (incomplete details, missing steps) are Level 3 issues\n  - Do NOT reject at Level 2 if ANY concrete specification was provided\n\n**Examples Showing the Boundary:**\n\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 works\" (nothing follows - Category A)\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 using vertical lines\" (type named, but NO concrete details - Category B)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 works: L₁: y=x, L₂: y=2x\" (has equations - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 via lines at points (1,1), (2,2), (3,3)\" (has points - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Sunny line through (n,1)\" (one point provided - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Three sunny lines cover 6 rightmost points\" (count+target - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n\n**WARNING - Common Misclassification:**\n- If solution says \"k=X works\" AND THEN provides equations/points/count → This is Category C (PASS Level 2)\n- Only reject at Level 2 if \"k=X works\" appears WITH NO concrete details following it\n- ONE point is better than ZERO points → proceed to Level 3 for completeness check\n\n**STEP 4: Handle Context-Dependent Claims (CRITICAL CALIBRATION)**\n\nSome mathematical claims are true in one context but false in another. Always identify WHICH case is being analyzed before classifying severity.\n\n**Example Pattern:**\n```\nClaim: \"The three rightmost columns force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\"\nContext A (k≤3): This claim is TRUE\nContext B (k≥4): This claim is FALSE\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≤3 case:\n  → Claim is TRUE in this context\n  → If scope not explicit: JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5, ACCEPTABLE)\n  → NOT a CRITICAL_ERROR just because it's false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must lie on at least one of the three lines.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\nBecause exactly two of the three lines must be sunny, there is precisely one non‑sunny line.  \nWe consider the three possible families of a non‑sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The non‑sunny line is horizontal.\n\nThe only horizontal lines meeting \\(\\mathcal P\\) are  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\qquad y=2,\\qquad y=3 .\n\\]\n\n*Case \\(y=1\\).*  \nIt contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nR_h=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line through two points of \\(R_h\\) is either vertical \\((x=1)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\); both are non‑sunny.  \nHence a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(R_h\\) unless it also passes through a point of the line \\(y=1\\).  \nIf a sunny line passes through a point of \\(R_h\\) and a point of \\(y=1\\), it covers exactly one point of \\(R_h\\) (the other point on the line is already on \\(y=1\\)).  \n\nWith only two sunny lines we can therefore cover at most two points of \\(R_h\\); at least one point of \\(R_h\\) stays uncovered.  \n\n*Case \\(y=2\\) or \\(y=3\\).*  \nEach of these lines contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\), leaving at least four points in the complement \\(R\\).  \nFor any two points of such an \\(R\\) the joining line is vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(R\\) unless it also meets the horizontal line \\(y=2\\) (or \\(y=3\\)).  \nThus two sunny lines can cover at most two points of \\(R\\), leaving at least two uncovered.  \n\nHence a horizontal non‑sunny line makes the required configuration impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The non‑sunny line is vertical.\n\nThe only vertical lines meeting \\(\\mathcal P\\) are  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad x=2,\\qquad x=3 .\n\\]\n\n*Case \\(x=1\\).*  \nIt contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nThe remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nR_v=\\{(2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line through two points of \\(R_v\\) is either horizontal \\((y=1)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\); both are non‑sunny.  \nThus a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(R_v\\) unless it also passes through a point of the line \\(x=1\\).  \nIf it does, the line still covers only one new point of \\(R_v\\).  \nWith two sunny lines we can cover at most two points of \\(R_v\\); at least one point remains uncovered.\n\n*Case \\(x=2\\) or \\(x=3\\).*  \nEach contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\), leaving at least four points to be covered by the two sunny lines.  \nExactly as in the previous case, any two of those points are joined by a non‑sunny line, so each sunny line can introduce at most one new point.  \nConsequently at least two points stay uncovered.\n\nThus a vertical non‑sunny line also leads to impossibility.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The non‑sunny line has slope \\(-1\\).\n\nLines of the form \\(x+y=c\\) meeting \\(\\mathcal P\\) are  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\qquad x+y=3,\\qquad x+y=4 .\n\\]\n\n*Case \\(x+y=2\\).*  \nIt contains only \\((1,1)\\); the other five points must be covered by two sunny lines.  \nAny two of those five points are joined by a vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line, i.e. a non‑sunny line.  \nHence each sunny line can introduce at most one new point, so at most two of the five points can be covered – contradiction.\n\n*Case \\(x+y=3\\).*  \nIt contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nR_{-1}=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAgain any line through two points of \\(R_{-1}\\) is non‑sunny.  \nA sunny line may meet the line \\(x+y=3\\) and a point of \\(R_{-1}\\); such a line still adds only one new point of \\(R_{-1}\\).  \nThus two sunny lines can cover at most two points of \\(R_{-1}\\), leaving at least two uncovered.\n\n*Case \\(x+y=4\\).*  \nIt contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nR_{4}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line through two points of \\(R_{4}\\) is vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\), i.e. non‑sunny.  \nA sunny line that also passes through a point of \\(x+y=4\\) can contain at most one point of \\(R_{4}\\).  \nFor instance, the sunny line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)) covers \\((1,2)\\); the sunny line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)) covers \\((2,1)\\).  \nThe point \\((1,1)\\) remains uncovered, and no other choice of two sunny lines can cover all three points of \\(R_{4}\\) for the same reason.\n\nHence a non‑sunny line of slope \\(-1\\) also makes the configuration impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible choice for the unique non‑sunny line forces at least one of the six required points to remain uncovered, there is **no** arrangement of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines that satisfies the covering condition for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, }k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The solution should explicitly state \"since p>3 is prime, it's not divisible by 3, so p≡1 or 2