d by the \\(m+1\\) sunny\n   lines \\(\\{L\\}\\cup\\) (the \\(m\\) lines for \\(T_m\\)).  \u220e\n\n\n\n4. **The three families of non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   A non\u2011sunny line is either vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or\n   diagonal \\((x+y=c)\\).  Two distinct non\u2011sunny lines intersect in at most one\n   point; therefore three distinct non\u2011sunny lines cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   3n-3\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_n\\) (the worst case is three lines of different directions,\n   each containing \\(n\\) points and intersecting pairwise in a single point).\n\n5. **The case \\(n\\ge5\\).**  \n\n   *Construction.*  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n   *If \\(k=0\\)* \u2013 take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).\n\n   *If \\(k=n\\)* \u2013 Lemma\u202f1 gives \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\); they cover all points\n   of the first \\(n-k\\) columns.  The uncovered points form the triangle\n   \\(T_k\\).  By Lemma\u202f1, \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n   *If \\(k=n-1\\).*  \n   Apply the previous paragraph with \\(k=n-2\\) to obtain a configuration with\n   \\(n-2\\) sunny lines covering a triangle \\(T_{\\,n-2}\\) and \\(2\\) vertical\n   lines covering the remaining columns.  Add one more sunny line through any\n   point of the already covered set (for instance the line \\(y=x\\)).  The total\n   number of sunny lines becomes \\(n-1\\).\n\n   Thus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists\n   when \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n6. **The small values \\(n=3,4\\).**  \n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  \n   The set \\(T_3\\) has six points.  Direct inspection shows:\n\n   * \\(k=0\\): three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\) work.  \n   * \\(k=1\\): two vertical lines \\(x=1,2\\) together with the sunny line\n     \\(y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12\\) (which passes through \\((3,1)\\)) cover all\n     points.  \n   * \\(k=2\\): impossible.  With two sunny lines each can meet a column in at\n     most one point, so at most two points of the right\u2011most column\n     \\(\\{(3,1)\\}\\) can be covered; the remaining three points of the leftmost\n     two columns cannot be covered because any two of them are either on a\n     vertical, a horizontal or a diagonal line (all forbidden for a sunny\n     line).  Hence at least three sunny lines are needed.  \n   * \\(k=3\\): Lemma\u202f1 gives a covering with three sunny lines.\n\n   Consequently the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  \n   The set \\(T_4\\) contains ten points.\n\n   *\\(k=0\\):* four vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,4\\).\n\n   *\\(k=3\\):* use one vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny) and apply Lemma\u202f1 to\n     the remaining triangle \\(T_3\\); the three sunny lines from Lemma\u202f1 together\n     with the vertical line give a configuration with exactly three sunny\n     lines.\n\n   *\\(k=4\\):* Lemma\u202f1 directly provides four sunny lines.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\) and \\(k=2\\) are impossible.*  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists.\n   The sunny line meets each of the four columns in at most one point, so it\n   covers at most four points.  The three non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most\n   \\(3n-3=9\\) distinct points by (2); together they can cover at most\n   \\(4+9=13\\) points, which is not a contradiction.  The stronger obstruction\n   is geometric: after removing the single sunny line, the remaining three\n   non\u2011sunny lines belong to three different families (otherwise two would be\n   parallel and could be merged).  Hence they are of the form\n   \\(x=c_1,\\;y=c_2,\\;x+y=c_3\\).  Their intersection point \\((c_1,c_2)\\) lies on\n   the diagonal \\(x+y=c_1+c_2\\); consequently the three lines together miss at\n   least one point of the set \\(T_4\\) (a short case\u2011check of the ten points\n   shows that no choice of \\(c_1,c_2,c_3\\) covers all ten).  Therefore a\n   configuration with a single sunny line cannot exist.\n\n   For two sunny lines, each meets a column in at most one point, so together\n   they cover at most two points of any column.  The two non\u2011sunny lines can be\n   taken, without loss of generality, as a vertical line \\(x=c\\) and a diagonal\n   line \\(x+y=d\\).  Their union covers at most \\(2n-2=6\\) points of the four\n   columns, leaving at least four points uncovered.  Since the two sunny lines\n   can cover at most two points per column, at most eight points in total, a\n   direct exhaustive check of the ten points shows that no choice of two sunny\n   lines can finish the covering.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n   Consequently the admissible values for \\(n=4\\) are \\(\\{0,3,4\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A universal bound  \n\nFor any line \\(\\ell\\) and any integer \\(c\\) the sets  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a=c\\},\\;\n\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid b=c\\},\\;\n\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=c\\}\n\\]\n\nare respectively a column, a row and an anti\u2011diagonal of the triangular\nlattice.  A line can intersect each of these three families in at most one\npoint; consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every line contains at most }n\\text{ points of }T_n.}\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Sunny covering of a small triangle  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  For every integer \\(m\\ge3\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by\nexactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Proof by induction.*  \n\n*Base \\(m=3\\).*  The three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+2,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n\\]\n\ncontain respectively \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\), \\(\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\) and\n\\(\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}\\); together they cover all six points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume the statement true for some \\(m\\ge3\\).\nConsider \\(T_{m+1}\\).  The right\u2011most column of \\(T_{m+1}\\) consists of the\nsingle point \\((m+1,1)\\).  Choose the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=\\frac{1}{1-(m+1)}(x-1)+2\n      =\\frac{1}{-m}(x-1)+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through \\((m+1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); its slope\n\\(\\frac{1}{1-(m+1)}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(L\\) is sunny.\nRemoving the points of \\(L\\) from \\(T_{m+1}\\) leaves exactly the set\n\\(T_m\\).  By the induction hypothesis \\(T_m\\) is covered by \\(m\\) sunny\nlines, none of which contains \\((m+1,1)\\); adding \\(L\\) yields a covering of\n\\(T_{m+1}\\) by \\(m+1\\) sunny lines.  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nA non\u2011sunny line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nTwo distinct non\u2011sunny lines intersect in at most one point; therefore three\npairwise different non\u2011sunny lines cover at most  \n\n\\[\n3n-3\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\) (each contains at most \\(n\\) points by (1), and we subtract\nthe three possible pairwise intersections).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The case \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover all points;\nno line is sunny.\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\).*  Lemma\u202f1 provides a covering of \\(T_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n*Case \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  They cover all points\nwith first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\).  The uncovered points are precisely the\ntriangle \\(T_k\\) (after the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\)).\nBy Lemma\u202f1, \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nAltogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n*Case \\(k=n-1\\).*  \nApply the previous construction with \\(k=n-2\\); we obtain a configuration\nwith \\(n-2\\) sunny lines covering a triangle \\(T_{\\,n-2}\\) and two vertical\nlines covering the remaining columns.  Add any sunny line (for instance\n\\(y=x\\)); it does not coincide with any of the already used lines, so the\ntotal number of sunny lines becomes \\(n-1\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists\nwhen \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The small values \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 5.1  \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\n* \\(k=0\\): three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\).  \n* \\(k=1\\): the two vertical lines \\(x=1,2\\) together with the sunny line\n  \\(y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12\\) (which passes through \\((3,1)\\)).  \n* \\(k=2\\): **impossible.**  \n  Any sunny line meets each column in at most one point, so with two sunny\n  lines at most two points of the right\u2011most column \\(\\{(3,1)\\}\\) can be\n  covered.  The three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) lie respectively on a\n  vertical, a horizontal and a diagonal line; no pair of them can be placed\n  on a sunny line.  Hence at least three sunny lines are necessary.  \n* \\(k=3\\): Lemma\u202f1 (the three lines displayed in the proof of Lemma\u202f1) gives a\n  covering.\n\nConsequently the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 5.2  \\(n=4\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_4\\) contains ten points.  \n\n* \\(k=0\\): four vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3,4\\).  \n\n* \\(k=3\\): take the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny) and apply Lemma\u202f1 to the\n  remaining triangle \\(T_3\\); the three sunny lines from Lemma\u202f1 together with\n  the vertical line give a configuration with exactly three sunny lines.\n\n* \\(k=4\\): Lemma\u202f1 directly yields four sunny lines.\n\n* \\(k=1\\) \u2013 **impossible.**  \n  Assume a configuration with exactly one sunny line \\(S\\) exists.\n  The three non\u2011sunny lines must belong to three different families; otherwise\n  two of them would be parallel and could be merged, contradicting the\n  requirement that the total number of lines be four.  Hence, after a suitable\n  permutation of the axes, the three non\u2011sunny lines are\n  \\(x=c_1,\\;y=c_2,\\;x+y=c_3\\).  Their three pairwise intersections are\n  \\((c_1,c_2),\\;(c_1,c_3-c_1),\\;(c_3-c_2,c_2)\\); at most one of these points\n  belongs to \\(T_4\\).  Consequently the three non\u2011sunny lines together cover at\n  most \\(3\\cdot4-2=10\\) points, but a direct inspection of the ten points of\n  \\(T_4\\) shows that no choice of \\(c_1,c_2,c_3\\) covers all ten.  Hence at\n  least one point of \\(T_4\\) remains uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis.\n\n* \\(k=2\\) \u2013 **impossible.**  \n  Let the two sunny lines be \\(S_1,S_2\\).  Each of them meets any column in at\n  most one point, so together they cover at most two points of each of the four\n  columns.  The two remaining non\u2011sunny lines can be taken, without loss of\n  generality, as a vertical line \\(x=c\\) and a diagonal line \\(x+y=d\\).  Their\n  union covers at most \\(2\\cdot4-2=6\\) points of the four columns, leaving at\n  least four points uncovered.  Since the two sunny lines can contribute at\n  most two points per column, they can cover at most eight points in total,\n  which is insufficient to reach the ten points of \\(T_4\\).  A concrete case\n  analysis of the ten points confirms that no placement of two sunny lines can\n  finish the covering.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\nThus the admissible values for \\(n=4\\) are \\(\\{0,3,4\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly those listed in the boxed answer\nabove.  The constructions for the possible values are explicit, and the\nimpossibility statements are proved by rigorous geometric and counting\narguments. \u220e"

