That's okay. So we could replace L1 with a sunny line that covers (2,1) and (3,2). But we also need to cover (1,5) and (2,3) and (3,1) maybe. Let's see if we can find a sunny line that covers (1,5) and (3,1) (that's L1). So we need L1 for those points. So we need both L1 and S3, but we only have two sunny lines. So we need to incorporate (2,1) into L1 or S3 into L1. L1 currently passes through (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). Could we modify L1 to also pass through (2,1)? That would require a line through (2,1) and (1,5) slope (5-1)/(1-2) = 4/(-1) = -4, which is sunny. That line would be y = -4x + 9? Let's compute: using (2,1): y - 1 = -4 (x - 2) => y = -4x + 8 + 1 = -4x + 9. At x=1, y = -4 + 9 = 5 (point (1,5)). At x=3, y = -12 + 9 = -3, not in T_5. So this line would cover (1,5) and (2,1) but not (3,1) or (2,3). So we would lose coverage of (3,1) and (2,3). Could we cover (3,1) and (2,3) with other lines? Possibly. Let's see if we can adjust the non-sunny lines to cover those. (3,1) could be covered by diag4 (x+y=4) or vertical x=3 (non-sunny). (2,3) could be covered by diag5 (x+y=5). So we could adjust non-sunny lines accordingly. Let's try a new configuration:\n\nGoal: Use two sunny lines: S1: line through (1,5) and (2,1) (slope -4). S2: line through (2,2) and (5,1) (slope -1/3). Then use three non-sunny lines to cover the rest.\n\nLet's define:\n\nSunny line S1: passes through (1,5) and (2,1). Equation: y = -4x + 9. It also passes through (3,? ) y = -12+9 = -3, not in T_5. So S1 covers (1,5) and (2,1).\n\nSunny line S2: passes through (2,2) and (5,1). Equation: y = -1/3 x + 8/3. It also passes through (3,? ) y = -1 + 8/3 = 5/3, not integer. So S2 covers (2,2) and (5,1).\n\nNow we need to cover the remaining points with three non-sunny lines. Let's list all points and mark those covered by S1 and S2:\n\nPoints:\n\n(1,1): not covered\n(1,2): not covered\n(1,3): not covered\n(1,4): not covered\n(1,5): S1\n(2,1): S1\n(2,2): S2\n(2,3): not covered\n(2,4): not covered\n(3,1): not 