r i = 1,...,k-1. For a given i, the line includes points (x, y) where y = x - i. For integer points, this includes points (i+1,1), (i+2,2), ..., up to (k, k-i). So L_i covers points (a,b) where a - b = i, i.e., a = b + i. So L_i covers points where a - b = i. For i from 1 to k-1, these are all points where a > b and a - b = i. So L_i covers all points below diagonal with a - b = i. Since a+b ≤ k+1, we need to check that for each i, the points (b+i, b) for b≥1 satisfy (b+i)+b ≤ k+1 => 2b + i ≤ k+1 => b ≤ (k+1 - i)/2. So for each i, the line L_i includes points (b+i, b) for b = 1,..., floor((k+1 - i)/2). So L_i covers all points below diagonal with a - b = i, up to the bound.\n\nNow what about points above diagonal (a < b)? Those are symmetric: we can use lines of slope 1 (diagonal) and maybe lines of slope -1? But slope -1 is not allowed. However, we can use lines of slope 1 that go through points (1,b) and (b,1)? Actually slope (1-b)/(b-1) = -(b-1)/(b-1) = -1, not allowed. So we need a different approach for points above diagonal.\n\nBut note that the set T_k is symmetric under swapping a and b. So we could also use lines of slope 1 (diagonal) and lines of slope 1? Actually lines of slope 1 are diagonal, they cover points where a = b. But we need to cover points where a < b. We could use lines of slope 1 that are offset: y = x + c for some constant c > 0. For integer points, line y = x + c includes points (a,b) where b = a + c. So these are points above diagonal with b - a = c. So we can use lines y = x + c for c = 1,...,k-1. However, slope 1 is allowed (sunny). So we can use lines y = x + c for c = 1,...,k-1. That would be k-1 lines. Combined with y = x (c=0) we have k lines. But we need to check if these lines cover all points of T_k. Let's examine.\n\nDefine lines:\n\n- For c = 0,...,k-1: L'_c: y = x + c (slope 1). For c=0, it's diagonal. For c>0, it's above diagonal.\n\nNow L'_c includes points (a,b) where b = a + c. For integer a≥1, b = a + c. The condition a+b