d \\(k-3\\) further sunny lines that miss the whole set \\(T_n\\); for instance, for any integer \\(M>n\\) the lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y = 2x + (M),\\; y = 3x + (M+1),\\dots , y = (k-2)x + (M+k-4)\n   \\]\n\n   are sunny and, because their ordinates exceed \\(n\\) for all \\(1\\le x\\le n\\), they contain no point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The total number of lines is \\(n\\) (the \\(k\\) sunny ones just described together with the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\)), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n\n   Hence for every \\(k\\ge 3\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n8. **Collecting the possibilities.**  \n   * \\(k=0\\) \u2013 possible (step\u202f4).  \n   * \\(k=1\\) \u2013 possible (step\u202f5).  \n   * \\(k=2\\) \u2013 impossible (step\u202f3).  \n   * \\(k\\ge 3\\) \u2013 possible (steps\u202f6\u20137).  \n\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k=0,\\;k=1,\\;\\text{or }3\\le k\\le n\\;}.\n   \\]\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound for a sunny line  \n\nA sunny line meets each vertical line \\(x=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n)\\) in at most one point; consequently it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nThe three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\;P_2=(1,2),\\;P_3=(2,1)\n\\]\n\nbelong to \\(T_n\\).  \nAny sunny line can contain at most one of them, because the three possible slopes\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\) are precisely the slopes of the lines joining any two of the points.\nHence with only two sunny lines at most two of \\(\\{P_1,P_2,P_3\\}\\) can be covered; the third\nmust be covered by a non\u2011sunny line.\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nEach such line can contain at most two of the three points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\); therefore one of the three points always remains uncovered, contradicting the covering requirement.  \nThus **\\(k=2\\) cannot occur** for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are non\u2011sunny, and a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe vertical lines cover every point with \\(a\\le n-1\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\((n,1)\\) and, when \\(n\\) is odd, \\(\\bigl(\\frac{n+1}{2},\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr)\\); both lie on \\(y=x\\).  \nThus exactly one sunny line suffices.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A three\u2011sunny\u2011line covering of \\(T_n\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x ,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -2x + (n+2) ,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{n+2}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes (\\(1,-2,-\\tfrac12\\)) differ from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the lines are sunny and distinct.\n\nFor a fixed column \\(a\\;(1\\le a\\le n)\\) the three lines give the candidate ordinates  \n\n\\[\nb_1=a,\\qquad b_2=n+2-2a,\\qquad b_3=\\frac{n+2-a}{2}.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nWe show that at least one of the numbers in (5.2) is an integer belonging to the interval\n\\([1,\\,n+1-a]\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).*  \nThen \\(b_1=a\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-a\\); consequently \\((a,b_1)\\in T_n\\) and lies on \\(L_1\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a>\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).*  \nHere \\(b_2=n+2-2a\\le0\\) and cannot belong to \\(T_n\\).  \nThe value \\(b_3=\\dfrac{n+2-a}{2}\\) is positive because \\(a\\le n\\).  \nMoreover  \n\n\\[\nb_3\\le n+1-a\\iff \\frac{n+2-a}{2}\\le n+1-a\\iff a\\le n,\n\\]\n\nso \\(b_3\\) never exceeds the allowed maximum.\n\nIf \\(n\\) and \\(a\\) have the same parity, then \\(b_3\\) is an integer and \\((a,b_3)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(L_3\\).\n\nIf the parity differs, then \\(b_3\\) is a half\u2011integer, but a direct computation shows that in this sub\u2011case  \n\n\\[\nb_2=n+2-2a=1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,1)\\in T_n\\) and lies on \\(L_2\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the three lines (5.1).  \nConsequently **three sunny lines are sufficient to cover the whole set**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  From three sunny lines to any \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \nStart with the three sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) from the previous paragraph; they already cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nAdd \\(k-3\\) further sunny lines that miss \\(T_n\\) completely.  \nFor example, choose an integer \\(M>n\\) and take the lines  \n\n\\[\ny = 2x + M,\\; y = 3x + (M+1),\\dots , y = (k-2)x + (M+k-4).\n\\]\n\nAll have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for every \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) their ordinates exceed \\(n\\), so they contain no point of \\(T_n\\).  \nThese \\(k-3\\) lines are distinct and sunny.\n\nFinally, use the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non\u2011sunny) to reach the total of \\(n\\) lines.  \nExactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Collection of all possible \\(k\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\) \u2013 possible (step\u202f3).  \n* \\(k=1\\) \u2013 possible (step\u202f4).  \n* \\(k=2\\) \u2013 impossible (step\u202f2).  \n* \\(k\\