,(2,2). But we only have 2 sunny lines allowed. So we cannot add a third sunny line. So this fails.\n\nThus for n=3, maybe k=2 is impossible. But we need to test for larger n. The solution's argument might be flawed but maybe the conclusion still holds for all n≥3. Let's examine more generally.\n\nWe need to see if there is any construction with exactly 2 sunny lines covering all points. The solution's argument uses the three points P1,P2,P3 and says each sunny line can contain at most one of them, so with two sunny lines we can cover at most two of them. The third must be covered by a non-sunny line. That's fine. Then they claim each non-sunny line can contain at most two of the three points, so one point always remains uncovered. But we have n-2 non-sunny lines, not just one. So we could use multiple non-sunny lines to cover the three points. However, we need to cover all points in T_n, not just these three. The argument might be that the non-sunny lines cannot cover all points because they are limited in some way. Let's examine the structure of T_n: It's a triangular region of integer points. Non-sunny lines are vertical (x=c), horizontal (y=c), or slope -1 (y = -x + d). Each such line can cover many points in T_n. For example, vertical line x=c covers points (c,b) for b from 1 to n+1-c. Horizontal line y=c covers points (a,c) for a from 1 to n+1-c. Slope -1 line y = -x + d covers points where a+b = d, i.e., points on a diagonal of the triangle. So non-sunny lines can cover many points.\n\nThus the impossibility of k=2 is not obvious. Let's try to construct a configuration for general n with exactly 2 sunny lines.\n\nWe need to cover all points in T_n using n lines, exactly 2 of which are sunny. The rest n-2 are non-sunny (vertical, horizontal, or slope -1). The non-sunny lines can be used to cover many points. The sunny lines can be used to cover points that cannot be covered by non-sunny lines? But any point can be covered by a vertical line x=a (non-sunny) or 